      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The cyst of Nuck is a swelling in the inguinal canal caused by a hydrocele of the round ligament. This abnormality is a result of the absence or incomplete obliteration of the processus vaginalis during foetal development."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE DESCRIPTION",
          "content": "A 44-year-old woman had suffered from a painful swelling in her right groin for 4 years. Extensive imaging and multiple punctures of the supposed cyst had no lasting results. Eventually, the swelling was identified as a cyst of Nuck, which was confirmed on operative exploration. After excision, the patient was relieved of all her symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The cyst of Nuck is a clinical diagnosis. Imaging the swelling may give a misleading result and cause a delay in treatment."
        }
      ]},
